ctions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} = \\dfrac{c}{d}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac ba =\\dfrac dc\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 23 = \\dfrac 2x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 32 = \\dfrac x2\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the multiplication principle:\n\n![](images/8e/8e7b2d027a761ba93f679cfb517709811938da412b06e59212391741b5660a2b.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059707 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-528cc092-636b-5bd7-9846-07908de9113d" "question-1f68ddcf-7f49-5e13-8fb1-2db21d4acf5a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059520 :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\dfrac 1{x+1} = 2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=-1$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} = \\dfrac{c}{d}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac ba =\\dfrac dc\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1{x+1} = 2 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac {x+1}{1} = \\dfrac 12\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to\n\n$$\nx+1 = \\dfrac 12 .\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the addition principle:\n\n![](images/d7/d7150c6093121f9f6dc0ecd9675d5be8f0855d339f3404d7a357875362d9990f.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059708 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b01bcc83-5d54-554e-8eb4-318389e1d142" "question-1a235d31-7002-5190-8de9-b78ca920ac5f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059521 :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\dfrac 1{x-2} = \\dfrac 3{5x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n- $x=2$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero, and\n\n- $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} = \\dfrac{c}{d}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac ba =\\dfrac dc\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1{x-2} = \\dfrac 3{5x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac {x-2}{1} = \\dfrac{5x}{3}\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to \n\n$$\nx-2 = \\dfrac{5x}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot (x-2) & =3\\cdot \\frac{5x}{3} \\\\ 3x-6 & =5x \\\\ 3x-5x & =6 \\\\ -2x & =6 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059709 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7e3eaae0-125c-585a-90f2-bbc8b4da35cd"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059522 :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\dfrac 1{2x-1} = \\dfrac{1}{x+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n- $x=\\dfrac 12$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero, and\n\n- $x=-1$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} = \\dfrac{c}{d}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac ba =\\dfrac dc\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1{2x-1} = \\dfrac{1}{x+1} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{2x-1}{1} = \\dfrac{x+1}1\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n2x-1 = x+1.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-1 & =x+1 \\\\ 2x-x & =1+1 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186060371 :question/problem "Solve the equation $-\\frac{1}{2x}=\\frac{3}{5}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{2x}{1}=\\frac{5}{3}\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n-2x=\\frac{5}{3}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x & =\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ (-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (-2x) & =(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot \\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\cancel{(-\\frac{1}{2})}\\cdot (\\cancel{-2}x) & =-\\frac{5}{6} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186058848 :answer-field/correct 17592186058853 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-9423a212-cd5b-5be8-a581-5c447b673232" :question/id #uuid "9423a212-cd5b-5be8-a581-5c447b673232" :question/math-academy-id "q-67407" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{4}{5}=\\frac{4}{3x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{4}=\\frac{3x}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4} & =\\frac{3x}{4} \\\\ 4\\cdot \\frac{5}{4} & =4\\cdot \\frac{3x}{4} \\\\ \\cancel{4}\\cdot \\frac{5}{\\cancel{4}} & =\\cancel{4}\\cdot \\frac{3x}{\\cancel{4}} \\\\ 5 & =3x \\\\ (\\frac{1}{3})\\cdot 5 & =(\\frac{1}{3})\\cdot (3x) \\\\ \\frac{5}{3} & =\\cancel{\\frac{1}{3}}\\cdot (\\cancel{3}x) \\\\ \\frac{5}{3} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the solution is $x=\\frac{5}{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d1354d1c-de34-5200-8c22-d682a6a448d0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d1354d1c-de34-5200-8c22-d682a6a448d0" :answer-field/id #uuid "d1354d1c-de34-5200-8c22-d682a6a448d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-62c728d8-29c1-5f90-a9bc-17eae1a900d6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-da440c13-f203-5583-aa96-40df058f74cd" "answer-c044e13e-665f-594c-8087-19c0c493d990" "answer-7733ee4f-eef5-5fd6-a757-f34e447b3828" "answer-80d7c6b7-28c9-5bea-bee5-7bb87f769d99" "answer-62c728d8-29c1-5f90-a9bc-17eae1a900d6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-da440c13-f203-5583-aa96-40df058f74cd" :answer/id #uuid "da440c13-f203-5583-aa96-40df058f74cd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c044e13e-665f-594c-8087-19c0c493d990" :answer/id #uuid "c044e13e-665f-594c-8087-19c0c493d990" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7733ee4f-eef5-5fd6-a757-f34e447b3828" :answer/id #uuid "7733ee4f-eef5-5fd6-a757-f34e447b3828" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-80d7c6b7-28c9-5bea-bee5-7bb87f769d99" :answer/id #uuid "80d7c6b7-28c9-5bea-bee5-7bb87f769d99" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-62c728d8-29c1-5f90-a9bc-17eae1a900d6" :answer/id #uuid "62c728d8-29c1-5f90-a9bc-17eae1a900d6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-528cc092-636b-5bd7-9846-07908de9113d" :question/id #uuid "528cc092-636b-5bd7-9846-07908de9113d" :question/math-academy-id "q-67416" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{2}{x-2}=4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=2$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the multiplication and the addition principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2)\\cdot (\\frac{x-2}{2}) & =2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\ (\\cancel{2})\\cdot (\\frac{x-2}{\\cancel{2}}) & =\\frac{2}{4} \\\\ x-2 & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x-2+2 & =\\frac{1}{2}+2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-44ae6554-557c-5337-b681-7691c583fdd1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-44ae6554-557c-5337-b681-7691c583fdd1" :answer-field/id #uuid "44ae6554-557c-5337-b681-7691c583fdd1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ebd6e3e2-7e5b-5a15-8a25-1e78f2e97115" :answer-field/choices ["answer-05d3d307-80c5-5100-8287-638a2bdecc60" "answer-33f90d44-4628-5b5e-b9c2-d33e90471828" "answer-5ef03d2c-7e33-54bd-ac16-3e50ecf310c8" "answer-ebd6e3e2-7e5b-5a15-8a25-1e78f2e97115" "answer-0cd1f877-23e8-59f2-a2aa-0744c5e7dd70"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-05d3d307-80c5-5100-8287-638a2bdecc60" :answer/id #uuid "05d3d307-80c5-5100-8287-638a2bdecc60" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-33f90d44-4628-5b5e-b9c2-d33e90471828" :answer/id #uuid "33f90d44-4628-5b5e-b9c2-d33e90471828" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5ef03d2c-7e33-54bd-ac16-3e50ecf310c8" :answer/id #uuid "5ef03d2c-7e33-54bd-ac16-3e50ecf310c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ebd6e3e2-7e5b-5a15-8a25-1e78f2e97115" :answer/id #uuid "ebd6e3e2-7e5b-5a15-8a25-1e78f2e97115" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0cd1f877-23e8-59f2-a2aa-0744c5e7dd70" :answer/id #uuid "0cd1f877-23e8-59f2-a2aa-0744c5e7dd70" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1f68ddcf-7f49-5e13-8fb1-2db21d4acf5a" :question/id #uuid "1f68ddcf-7f49-5e13-8fb1-2db21d4acf5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-67419" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{2}{5}=\\frac{1}{x+3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=-3$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{x+3}{1}\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2}=x+3\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the addition principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2} & =x+3 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}-3 & =x+3-3 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the solution is $x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-62a98138-ae00-5cf7-a233-4b12748aeb11"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-62a98138-ae00-5cf7-a233-4b12748aeb11" :answer-field/id #uuid "62a98138-ae00-5cf7-a233-4b12748aeb11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-095c670a-25a7-50cf-a6e1-36f32e4baf89" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e7cd306d-6fe0-5878-8f56-d8dc223f7387" "answer-095c670a-25a7-50cf-a6e1-36f32e4baf89" "answer-7c467b23-4da6-5c03-a3aa-f8f4d7260107" "answer-709e927c-126c-5ca0-b3f5-2687d69d3389" "answer-b34d2f08-dbcb-50dc-b729-b8924b43ec51"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e7cd306d-6fe0-5878-8f56-d8dc223f7387" :answer/id #uuid "e7cd306d-6fe0-5878-8f56-d8dc223f7387" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-095c670a-25a7-50cf-a6e1-36f32e4baf89" :answer/id #uuid "095c670a-25a7-50cf-a6e1-36f32e4baf89" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7c467b23-4da6-5c03-a3aa-f8f4d7260107" :answer/id #uuid "7c467b23-4da6-5c03-a3aa-f8f4d7260107" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-709e927c-126c-5ca0-b3f5-2687d69d3389" :answer/id #uuid "709e927c-126c-5ca0-b3f5-2687d69d3389" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b34d2f08-dbcb-50dc-b729-b8924b43ec51" :answer/id #uuid "b34d2f08-dbcb-50dc-b729-b8924b43ec51" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186060377 :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{1}{x+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=-1$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{x+1}{1}\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}=x+1\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the addition principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & =x+1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}-1 & =x+1-1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the solution is $x=-\\frac{1}{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186058884 :answer-field/correct 17592186058889 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-b01bcc83-5d54-554e-8eb4-318389e1d142" :question/id #uuid "b01bcc83-5d54-554e-8eb4-318389e1d142" :question/math-academy-id "q-67422" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{6}{x+2}=\\frac{3}{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n- $x=-2$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+2}{6}=\\frac{x}{3}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator $6.$ The above equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+2}{6}=\\frac{2x}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot \\frac{x+2}{6} & =6\\cdot \\frac{2x}{6} \\\\ x+2 & =2x \\\\ 2 & =2x-x \\\\ 2 & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the solution is $x=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e4d1887c-0c5d-5ac5-9c0f-77c952d10182"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e4d1887c-0c5d-5ac5-9c0f-77c952d10182" :answer-field/id #uuid "e4d1887c-0c5d-5ac5-9c0f-77c952d10182" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2f7989a1-6ce6-581c-a185-11e1a6852423" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0e09809f-1ac1-5163-95fe-56a6c3cca096" "answer-94f3bb0d-e212-55c1-a64b-282f04cc5137" "answer-2f7989a1-6ce6-581c-a185-11e1a6852423" "answer-78dcdf47-9629-5ab8-a15d-6bafe37b89e3" "answer-1702597c-5660-544c-a89c-cba9f00560d7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0e09809f-1ac1-5163-95fe-56a6c3cca096" :answer/id #uuid "0e09809f-1ac1-5163-95fe-56a6c3cca096" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-94f3bb0d-e212-55c1-a64b-282f04cc5137" :answer/id #uuid "94f3bb0d-e212-55c1-a64b-282f04cc5137" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2f7989a1-6ce6-581c-a185-11e1a6852423" :answer/id #uuid "2f7989a1-6ce6-581c-a185-11e1a6852423" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-78dcdf47-9629-5ab8-a15d-6bafe37b89e3" :answer/id #uuid "78dcdf47-9629-5ab8-a15d-6bafe37b89e3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1702597c-5660-544c-a89c-cba9f00560d7" :answer/id #uuid "1702597c-5660-544c-a89c-cba9f00560d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1a235d31-7002-5190-8de9-b78ca920ac5f" :question/id #uuid "1a235d31-7002-5190-8de9-b78ca920ac5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-67425" :question/problem "Solve the equation $-\\frac{3}{x}=\\frac{9}{3x-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n- $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{x}{3}=\\frac{3x-1}{9}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator $9.$ The above equation becomes\n\n$$\n-\\frac{3x}{9}=\\frac{3x-1}{9}.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-9)\\cdot \\frac{3x}{-9} & =(-9)\\cdot \\frac{3x-1}{9} \\\\ 3x & =(-1)\\cdot (3x-1) \\\\ 3x & =1-3x \\\\ 3x+3x & =1-3x+3x \\\\ 6x & =1 \\\\ (\\frac{1}{6})\\cdot 6x & =(\\frac{1}{6})\\cdot 1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fbaaab81-294e-5a37-9fb1-b73c2d58a5c3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fbaaab81-294e-5a37-9fb1-b73c2d58a5c3" :answer-field/id #uuid "fbaaab81-294e-5a37-9fb1-b73c2d58a5c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-dcb302e9-c203-5981-98a6-1abb4868178a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e0db71b8-ad73-5cce-b817-b4e5c4f03d39" "answer-ecb895ed-3b69-53d2-9ba7-f858762d5ad6" "answer-55a6fcca-aad9-5ad1-a121-fdaf665d5aad" "answer-d92f70d4-3067-5353-97b7-e64c25a54de0" "answer-dcb302e9-c203-5981-98a6-1abb4868178a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0db71b8-ad73-5cce-b817-b4e5c4f03d39" :answer/id #uuid "e0db71b8-ad73-5cce-b817-b4e5c4f03d39" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ecb895ed-3b69-53d2-9ba7-f858762d5ad6" :answer/id #uuid "ecb895ed-3b69-53d2-9ba7-f858762d5ad6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-55a6fcca-aad9-5ad1-a121-fdaf665d5aad" :answer/id #uuid "55a6fcca-aad9-5ad1-a121-fdaf665d5aad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d92f70d4-3067-5353-97b7-e64c25a54de0" :answer/id #uuid "d92f70d4-3067-5353-97b7-e64c25a54de0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dcb302e9-c203-5981-98a6-1abb4868178a" :answer/id #uuid "dcb302e9-c203-5981-98a6-1abb4868178a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186060374 :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{6}{x-3}=\\frac{2}{x+6}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n- $x=3$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=-6$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{6}=\\frac{x+6}{2}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator, $6.$ The equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{6}=\\frac{3x+18}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot \\frac{x-3}{6} & =6\\cdot \\frac{3x+18}{6} \\\\ x-3 & =3x+18 \\\\ x-3x & =18+3 \\\\ -2x & =21 \\\\ (-\\frac{1}{2})(-2x) & =(-\\frac{1}{2})21 \\\\ (-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{2}})(-\\cancel{2}x) & =(-\\frac{1}{2})21 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{21}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186058866 :answer-field/correct 17592186058870 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-7e3eaae0-125c-585a-90f2-bbc8b4da35cd" :question/id #uuid "7e3eaae0-125c-585a-90f2-bbc8b4da35cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-106354" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{x+5}=\\frac{3}{2x+12}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n- $x=-5$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=-6$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+5}{1}=\\frac{2x+12}{3}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator, $3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x+15}{3}=\\frac{2x+12}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot (\\frac{3x+15}{3}) & =3\\cdot (\\frac{2x+12}{3}) \\\\ 3x+15 & =2x+12 \\\\ 3x-2x & =12-15 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7c930558-3f13-59ed-984d-aa81dfac2ac2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7c930558-3f13-59ed-984d-aa81dfac2ac2" :answer-field/id #uuid "7c930558-3f13-59ed-984d-aa81dfac2ac2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7ff51bff-7de4-5253-9d95-cedff81705fd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8d0aa041-1831-5a03-87fe-4c02c07d7e82" "answer-7ff51bff-7de4-5253-9d95-cedff81705fd" "answer-30bc81e6-8fff-54e2-ac15-49e3fb530116" "answer-a0dc91f3-4ee3-57a4-8547-157373bab8fe" "answer-d8fd723a-f248-5a39-96ba-76e1a1a6935a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8d0aa041-1831-5a03-87fe-4c02c07d7e82" :answer/id #uuid "8d0aa041-1831-5a03-87fe-4c02c07d7e82" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ff51bff-7de4-5253-9d95-cedff81705fd" :answer/id #uuid "7ff51bff-7de4-5253-9d95-cedff81705fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-30bc81e6-8fff-54e2-ac15-49e3fb530116" :answer/id #uuid "30bc81e6-8fff-54e2-ac15-49e3fb530116" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a0dc91f3-4ee3-57a4-8547-157373bab8fe" :answer/id #uuid "a0dc91f3-4ee3-57a4-8547-157373bab8fe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d8fd723a-f248-5a39-96ba-76e1a1a6935a" :answer/id #uuid "d8fd723a-f248-5a39-96ba-76e1a1a6935a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186060937 :tutorial/content "Remember that to solve a rational equation, such as the one below, we can use cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x} & =\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 5\\cdot 2 & =3\\cdot x \\\\ 10 & =3x \\\\ \\frac{10}{3} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, there is also another method that we can use, known as the **flip method,** which involves taking the reciprocal of both sides of an equation. So, when an equation involves two fractions, we can \"flip\" them as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} = \\dfrac{c}{d}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac ba =\\dfrac dc\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nThe flip method is useful because it allows us to remove a variable from a denominator. For example, applying the flip method, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}2}{\\color{blue}x}=\\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{\\color{red}5}  \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\color{blue}x}{\\color{red}2} = \\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{\\color{blue}3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the resulting equation using the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot \\frac{x}{2} & =2\\cdot \\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\cancel{2}\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\cancel{2}} & =2\\cdot \\frac{5}{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{10}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059941 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166736 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-951aecd2-9a03-5921-a647-8badf487101e" "question-7c63f903-029e-5df7-9333-d7481c32658b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-951aecd2-9a03-5921-a647-8badf487101e" :question/id #uuid "951aecd2-9a03-5921-a647-8badf487101e" :question/math-academy-id "q-224995" :question/problem "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼\\text{Po}(9)$ given below, find the value of $P(X\\le 7).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.0212\\,$ | $\\,0.0550\\,$ | $\\,0.1157\\,$ | $\\,0.2068\\,$ | $\\,0.3239\\,$ | $\\,0.4557\\,$ | $\\,0.5874\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 7) & =F(7) \\\\  & =0.3239.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-03d92468-cb05-508e-bcd0-42d2b482d3c9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-03d92468-cb05-508e-bcd0-42d2b482d3c9" :answer-field/id #uuid "03d92468-cb05-508e-bcd0-42d2b482d3c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-072af78c-0cf5-586f-bc53-ad8197623ca0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a6c5ad47-e307-560d-aa30-f635fcf6ddd7" "answer-23093e91-9f8a-526f-b9fe-a4c366c326dc" "answer-072af78c-0cf5-586f-bc53-ad8197623ca0" "answer-fe79ce86-a670-57ab-ab9f-302046099afd" "answer-ddc0b37a-e911-5ce6-b543-981451964978"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a6c5ad47-e307-560d-aa30-f635fcf6ddd7" :answer/id #uuid "a6c5ad47-e307-560d-aa30-f635fcf6ddd7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.1171" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-23093e91-9f8a-526f-b9fe-a4c366c326dc" :answer/id #uuid "23093e91-9f8a-526f-b9fe-a4c366c326dc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2068" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-072af78c-0cf5-586f-bc53-ad8197623ca0" :answer/id #uuid "072af78c-0cf5-586f-bc53-ad8197623ca0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.3239" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fe79ce86-a670-57ab-ab9f-302046099afd" :answer/id #uuid "fe79ce86-a670-57ab-ab9f-302046099afd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.4557" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ddc0b37a-e911-5ce6-b543-981451964978" :answer/id #uuid "ddc0b37a-e911-5ce6-b543-981451964978" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.5307" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7c63f903-029e-5df7-9333-d7481c32658b" :question/id #uuid "7c63f903-029e-5df7-9333-d7481c32658b" :question/math-academy-id "q-224997" :question/problem "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼\\text{Po}(8)$ given below, compute $P(X<9).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ | $9$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.0996\\,$ | $\\,0.1912\\,$ | $\\,0.3134\\,$ | $\\,0.4530\\,$ | $\\,0.5925\\,$ | $\\,0.7166\\,$ | $\\,0.8159\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<9) & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,…,8\\}) \\\\  & =P(X\\le 8) \\\\  & =F(8) \\\\  & =0.5925.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b0b47d28-52ea-5db5-8d68-c29eb95437d3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b0b47d28-52ea-5db5-8d68-c29eb95437d3" :answer-field/id #uuid "b0b47d28-52ea-5db5-8d68-c29eb95437d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b364a7c4-21e7-540e-8e24-cda36eac8906" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e4a0a61b-0e09-5891-9fdf-a1c86f0ad38d" "answer-b364a7c4-21e7-540e-8e24-cda36eac8906" "answer-bf85c091-12aa-55ce-9c65-94896b0eada1" "answer-871b12f6-b300-5aad-9c37-0f6b3ba75025" "answer-f31f3176-4cec-53f7-9c8b-c339b62c86e0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e4a0a61b-0e09-5891-9fdf-a1c86f0ad38d" :answer/id #uuid "e4a0a61b-0e09-5891-9fdf-a1c86f0ad38d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.1241" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b364a7c4-21e7-540e-8e24-cda36eac8906" :answer/id #uuid "b364a7c4-21e7-540e-8e24-cda36eac8906" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.5925" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bf85c091-12aa-55ce-9c65-94896b0eada1" :answer/id #uuid "bf85c091-12aa-55ce-9c65-94896b0eada1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.7166" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-871b12f6-b300-5aad-9c37-0f6b3ba75025" :answer/id #uuid "871b12f6-b300-5aad-9c37-0f6b3ba75025" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.7664" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f31f3176-4cec-53f7-9c8b-c339b62c86e0" :answer/id #uuid "f31f3176-4cec-53f7-9c8b-c339b62c86e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.8159" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166737 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3df054eb-2d42-57cd-ac48-5335659c80b9" "question-b92762e3-576d-55cb-b009-a97e0be6338a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-3df054eb-2d42-57cd-ac48-5335659c80b9" :question/id #uuid "3df054eb-2d42-57cd-ac48-5335659c80b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-225055" :question/problem "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼\\text{Po}(5)$ given below, compute $P(2\\le X\\le 4).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.0067\\,$ | $\\,0.0404\\,$ | $\\,0.1247\\,$ | $\\,0.2650\\,$ | $\\,0.4405\\,$ | $\\,0.6160\\,$ | $\\,0.7622\\,$ |\n\n$P(2\\le X\\le 4)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(4) & =P(X\\le 4) \\\\  & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,2,3,4\\}) \\\\  & =P(X\\in \\{0,1\\})+P(X\\in \\{2,3,4\\}) \\\\  & =P(X\\le 1)+P(2\\le X\\le 4) \\\\  & =F(1)+P(2\\le X\\le 4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2\\le X\\le 4) & =F(4)-F(1) \\\\  & =0.4405-0.0404 \\\\  & =0.4001.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8bb07aaf-fd25-5aa9-a148-43a824ecca08"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8bb07aaf-fd25-5aa9-a148-43a824ecca08" :answer-field/id #uuid "8bb07aaf-fd25-5aa9-a148-43a824ecca08" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-79a1b4b4-0c8f-509b-96be-e81e96d0cf38" :answer-field/choices ["answer-79a1b4b4-0c8f-509b-96be-e81e96d0cf38"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-79a1b4b4-0c8f-509b-96be-e81e96d0cf38" :answer/id #uuid "79a1b4b4-0c8f-509b-96be-e81e96d0cf38" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.4001" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b92762e3-576d-55cb-b009-a97e0be6338a" :question/id #uuid "b92762e3-576d-55cb-b009-a97e0be6338a" :question/math-academy-id "q-225067" :question/problem "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼\\text{Po}(4)$ given below, compute $P(3\\le X<6).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.0916\\,$ | $\\,0.2381\\,$ | $\\,0.4335\\,$ | $\\,0.6288\\,$ | $\\,0.7851\\,$ | $\\,0.8893\\,$ | $\\,0.9489\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(5) & =P(X<6) \\\\  & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,2,3,4,5\\}) \\\\  & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,2\\})+P(X\\in \\{3,4,5\\}) \\\\  & =P(X\\le 2)+P(3\\le X<6) \\\\  & =F(2)+P(3\\le X<6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3\\le X<6) & =F(5)-F(2) \\\\  & =0.7851-0.2381 \\\\  & =0.5470.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a20cf601-9154-52ab-ba75-6f001245e333"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a20cf601-9154-52ab-ba75-6f001245e333" :answer-field/id #uuid "a20cf601-9154-52ab-ba75-6f001245e333" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d834d903-ffee-58a0-92db-9ab0051f1e9d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d2d579dc-32ed-5798-98c2-1ace09760f2a" "answer-5577f8d1-9f94-5c6f-ba9c-92f1736f818d" "answer-d834d903-ffee-58a0-92db-9ab0051f1e9d" "answer-380c1b05-6c1c-5ffd-bc5c-a04c05c5c687" "answer-6e80960c-3886-58dd-b64a-4e2557138902"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d2d579dc-32ed-5798-98c2-1ace09760f2a" :answer/id #uuid "d2d579dc-32ed-5798-98c2-1ace09760f2a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.3516" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5577f8d1-9f94-5c6f-ba9c-92f1736f818d" :answer/id #uuid "5577f8d1-9f94-5c6f-ba9c-92f1736f818d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.4558" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d834d903-ffee-58a0-92db-9ab0051f1e9d" :answer/id #uuid "d834d903-ffee-58a0-92db-9ab0051f1e9d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.5470" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-380c1b05-6c1c-5ffd-bc5c-a04c05c5c687" :answer/id #uuid "380c1b05-6c1c-5ffd-bc5c-a04c05c5c687" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.6288" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6e80960c-3886-58dd-b64a-4e2557138902" :answer/id #uuid "6e80960c-3886-58dd-b64a-4e2557138902" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.6512" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166738 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-5f03e36d-2709-59cb-981a-892881905e64"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-5f03e36d-2709-59cb-981a-892881905e64" :question/id #uuid "5f03e36d-2709-59cb-981a-892881905e64" :question/math-academy-id "q-225087" :question/problem "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼\\text{Po}(3.6)$ given below, compute $P(X\\ge 5).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.5152\\,$ | $\\,0.7064\\,$ | $\\,0.8441\\,$ | $\\,0.9267\\,$ | $\\,0.9692\\,$ | $\\,0.9883\\,$ | $\\,0.9960\\,$ |\n\n$P(X\\ge 5)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nTo solve the given problem, we can use the rule\n\n$$\nP(X>a)=1-P(X\\le a)=1-F(a).\n$$\n\nNow, note that \n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 5)=P(X>4).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 5) & =P(X>4) \\\\  & =1-P(X\\le 4) \\\\  & =1-F(4) \\\\  & =1-0.7064 \\\\  & =0.2936.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7dda7cdb-4dec-5905-b4f9-bd936abe49ba"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7dda7cdb-4dec-5905-b4f9-bd936abe49ba" :answer-field/id #uuid "7dda7cdb-4dec-5905-b4f9-bd936abe49ba" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-d77a7660-717e-5f04-b7b5-229f67d8428a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d77a7660-717e-5f04-b7b5-229f67d8428a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d77a7660-717e-5f04-b7b5-229f67d8428a" :answer/id #uuid "d77a7660-717e-5f04-b7b5-229f67d8428a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2936" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-a0a97922-c29e-5eb9-a00b-a08031d2cade" :activity/id #uuid "a0a97922-c29e-5eb9-a00b-a08031d2cade" :activity/title "The CDF of the Poisson Distribution" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049899 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-8fc62da4-3a4c-5a53-bfc2-793fee1d81cb" "step-b2ab6c64-abb3-5af6-ae48-fa7ca76e80c7" "step-84e1ee1a-4b89-5aa4-b30a-3a5d79ef978f" "step-21d06