ure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5fdf560a-a525-5d1b-81e2-dd9b85199415" :answer/id #uuid "5fdf560a-a525-5d1b-81e2-dd9b85199415" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{25}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6cee1bab-f9ff-5b00-b291-ba94e9ca68c1" :answer/id #uuid "6cee1bab-f9ff-5b00-b291-ba94e9ca68c1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{27}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-05b1ed8b-6f64-5b7a-93ac-e69ad236eee5" :answer/id #uuid "05b1ed8b-6f64-5b7a-93ac-e69ad236eee5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{33}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186155402 :answer-field/choices 17592186155403] [:db/retract 17592186155402 :answer-field/choices 17592186155404] [:db/retract 17592186155402 :answer-field/choices 17592186155405] [:db/retract 17592186155402 :answer-field/choices 17592186155406] [:db/retract 17592186155402 :answer-field/choices 17592186155407] {:db/id 17592186157455 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7dd414c2-6c98-512b-8e09-cef5e3ee65c4"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157456 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2f37a13c-db81-5c51-b1fe-cf49ee77cc51" "question-ca23d1f9-e0ba-5c4f-a4c8-1fb3f4fded3f" "question-74b05c82-78aa-5425-a974-d8adb06f503c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157457 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a6116c6c-0d2d-5d26-9e6b-b6dd0de87989" "question-16980bbd-b929-5d3b-bedf-b283c32f0104"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157755 :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y=x{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, which of the following is a suitable ansatz for the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186155402 :answer-field/correct "answer-05e79502-7a84-58cf-b957-5624d1fb7a54" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a11e41b1-1554-538d-b4f1-99a16bb0736c" "answer-05e79502-7a84-58cf-b957-5624d1fb7a54" "answer-3ce4f4c2-afcf-522c-ac45-25a5710f32ef" "answer-838a2a65-1a47-578c-aaaf-e0cbb0d4ea24" "answer-a2ba09ac-3cee-5af2-854b-5cc721d6c468"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a11e41b1-1554-538d-b4f1-99a16bb0736c" :answer/id #uuid "a11e41b1-1554-538d-b4f1-99a16bb0736c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-05e79502-7a84-58cf-b957-5624d1fb7a54" :answer/id #uuid "05e79502-7a84-58cf-b957-5624d1fb7a54" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3ce4f4c2-afcf-522c-ac45-25a5710f32ef" :answer/id #uuid "3ce4f4c2-afcf-522c-ac45-25a5710f32ef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-838a2a65-1a47-578c-aaaf-e0cbb0d4ea24" :answer/id #uuid "838a2a65-1a47-578c-aaaf-e0cbb0d4ea24" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a2ba09ac-3cee-5af2-854b-5cc721d6c468" :answer/id #uuid "a2ba09ac-3cee-5af2-854b-5cc721d6c468" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{6x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7dd414c2-6c98-512b-8e09-cef5e3ee65c4" :question/id #uuid "7dd414c2-6c98-512b-8e09-cef5e3ee65c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-337257" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4y=5x-2.\n$$\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, which of the following is a suitable ansatz for the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fbd9e9ed-c643-53cb-b091-8e25e3819c29"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fbd9e9ed-c643-53cb-b091-8e25e3819c29" :answer-field/id #uuid "fbd9e9ed-c643-53cb-b091-8e25e3819c29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e5472203-9fba-5bba-9c12-92ddfa087678" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b41828ce-a30a-50d1-98f4-7686c3dfd227" "answer-661a4ab6-2aee-5700-ab4e-f425ea0e02c5" "answer-5c5276c3-cc24-5191-8280-b2c709babf8d" "answer-e5472203-9fba-5bba-9c12-92ddfa087678" "answer-d799b5e6-0c10-503f-9f9b-c433d246760b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b41828ce-a30a-50d1-98f4-7686c3dfd227" :answer/id #uuid "b41828ce-a30a-50d1-98f4-7686c3dfd227" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-661a4ab6-2aee-5700-ab4e-f425ea0e02c5" :answer/id #uuid "661a4ab6-2aee-5700-ab4e-f425ea0e02c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5c5276c3-cc24-5191-8280-b2c709babf8d" :answer/id #uuid "5c5276c3-cc24-5191-8280-b2c709babf8d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e5472203-9fba-5bba-9c12-92ddfa087678" :answer/id #uuid "e5472203-9fba-5bba-9c12-92ddfa087678" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d799b5e6-0c10-503f-9f9b-c433d246760b" :answer/id #uuid "d799b5e6-0c10-503f-9f9b-c433d246760b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{5x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2f37a13c-db81-5c51-b1fe-cf49ee77cc51" :question/id #uuid "2f37a13c-db81-5c51-b1fe-cf49ee77cc51" :question/math-academy-id "q-352901" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-x})}^{′}+(u{e}^{-x}) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-x}-u{e}^{-x}+u{e}^{-x} & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-x}-\\cancel{u{e}^{-x}}+\\cancel{u{e}^{-x}} & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-x} & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x \\\\ {u}^{′} & =3{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =3{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 3{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =\\int 3{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =\\int 3{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {e}^{-x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}\\int 3{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-81614ff3-0cbe-52bd-8674-7e06ead3791c" "answer-field-38fcdd80-1693-5154-8b83-577460a5519c" "answer-field-270268cf-3633-5ce9-82a2-e7b317c9ea4f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-81614ff3-0cbe-52bd-8674-7e06ead3791c" :answer-field/id #uu