er-field-e8142952-f5ec-5c5e-80de-d77973e116bf" :answer-field/id #uuid "e8142952-f5ec-5c5e-80de-d77973e116bf" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-7eb41de7-3db6-5b13-bd58-a86afdf1107d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9a58fb98-9012-575d-a382-7233455d76fb" "answer-4334b854-8407-53fa-aaf6-9cf5934370e4" "answer-7eb41de7-3db6-5b13-bd58-a86afdf1107d" "answer-d0c274a9-0726-5088-99bc-154319b4baa4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9a58fb98-9012-575d-a382-7233455d76fb" :answer/id #uuid "9a58fb98-9012-575d-a382-7233455d76fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{-2x}\\int {e}^{-2x}(x+3)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4334b854-8407-53fa-aaf6-9cf5934370e4" :answer/id #uuid "4334b854-8407-53fa-aaf6-9cf5934370e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{-2x}\\int {e}^{2x}(3x+1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7eb41de7-3db6-5b13-bd58-a86afdf1107d" :answer/id #uuid "7eb41de7-3db6-5b13-bd58-a86afdf1107d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{2x}\\int {e}^{-2x}(3x+1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d0c274a9-0726-5088-99bc-154319b4baa4" :answer/id #uuid "d0c274a9-0726-5088-99bc-154319b4baa4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{2x}\\int {e}^{-2x}(3x-1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-74b05c82-78aa-5425-a974-d8adb06f503c" :question/id #uuid "74b05c82-78aa-5425-a974-d8adb06f503c" :question/math-academy-id "q-337372" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y={e}^{-5x}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-2x})}^{′}+2(u{e}^{-2x}) & ={e}^{-5x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-2x}-2u{e}^{-2x}+2u{e}^{-2x} & ={e}^{-5x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-2x}-\\cancel{2u{e}^{-2x}}+\\cancel{2u{e}^{-2x}} & ={e}^{-5x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & ={e}^{-5x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & ={e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & ={e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-2x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-2x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3x}\\cdot {e}^{-2x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5141fac0-843c-524b-87af-8204b1e1c146" "answer-field-abd0e436-6a81-5d55-beca-423ce27fb414" "answer-field-5c6771f7-7fc1-5712-b47f-1eba427abfde"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answ